  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1106 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nRelatively cooler weather will remain across the Mid-South into\nthe early portions of this week as an area of surface high\npressure over the Great Lakes continues to push cooler air south.\nSo, temperatures on Monday will remain quite moderated and around\nnormal with lows in the 40s and highs in the 70s. Evening upper\nair observations from both LZK and OHX show the presence of very\ndry air not only at the surface, but aloft. Diurnal mixing of\nthis drier layer is expected to bring dewpoints well below the\ncurrent NBM forecast (~40 F) down into the middle 30s throughout\nthe entire region. Given the lack of appreciable rainfall over\nthe past month, or potentially longer in some places, fuels are\nvery dry and will lead to elevated fire danger and the potential\nfor spreading wildfires across the Mid-South through Monday\nafternoon.\n\nUpper ridging will gradually build in through the middle of the\nweek with a ridge axis from Texas through Minnesota emerging from\nthe upper pattern. Ironically, the surface high over the Ohio\nRiver Valley and Midwest will weaken with time, or at least the\npressure gradient over the southern CONUS will drop to negligible\nlevels. This will effectively nullify any advection of cooler air\nduring daytime hours, and with no moisture return on the horizon,\nwill lead to drastic diurnal temperature swings. Lows on Tuesday\nare currently forecast to be between 40 and 50 degrees, jumping\nnearly 30 to 35 degrees by the afternoon hours with highs\nprojected in the middle 70s and low 80s. Models are in good\nagreement that this pattern will likely persist through the rest\nof this week, leading to a gradual warming trend to above-average\nhighs alongside no rainfall. Fire weather danger will likely\nremain a concern as well with no moisture relief or precipitation\nto wet fuels.\n\nBy next weekend, ensembles are in good agreement that the upper\nridge axis will have only moved slightly east, oriented from\nTexas through Michigan Saturday. Spread does increase into\nSunday, but even then, the mean solution is for residual upper\nridging to remain over the Mid-South. Therefore, it seems likely\nthat more dry weather and above-average temperatures will persist\nthrough this weekend and the end of the forecast period.",
